"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3140],{3140:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"05-vyhod-iz-dolgov","title":"Lesson 05. Leaving debt behind: A plan without heroics","sourceFingerprint":"af48910ea6383f3ceff97b37f59e72439e7446ad0dfca3e02b04fad6830c7c3f","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"## Leaving debt behind"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"A plan without heroics"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"Many open this module already in debt, sometimes with several balances or no idea where to begin. PSB analysts estimated in summer 2026 that around 11.3 million Russians simultaneously serviced 3 or more loans, with average debt around 1.6 million RUB. Debt is escaped through an order of actions, with little need for heroism or willpower. Here is the order."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"### Step 1. The full picture"},{"id":"b005","markdown":"Many do not know exactly how much they owe or to whom: one bank’s card, another’s instalments and a loan they would rather forget. Request credit history through Gosuslugi. Each credit bureau must provide it free 2 times yearly. It lists everything in your name, sometimes including fraudulent loans. Then combine every debt in one table:"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"| Lender | Outstanding balance | Full cost or rate | Monthly payment | End date |\\nCalculate total debt and monthly payments. Divide payments by income for the debt-burden ratio from the previous lesson. It is uncomfortable, but afterwards you control the situation."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"### Step 2. Turn off the tap"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"While repaying old debt, no new debt should appear. Otherwise you are running in place."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"- Set a credit restriction through Gosuslugi, at least for online and microfinance loans."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"- Delete saved credit cards from marketplaces and delivery apps."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"- Do not borrow to repay old debt. The sole exception is refinancing at a lower rate, discussed below."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"- Remember leaks from lesson 2. Every thousand saved now works as early repayment."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"### Step 3. A 1-month buffer"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"Saving while owing at 40% may seem strange. But with no reserve, the first breakdown, illness or unexpected bill sends you back to the card and erases progress. First build a small buffer of 1 month’s essential expenses in a savings account. Once complete, all spare money goes to debt. The full cushion comes later in lesson 6."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"### Step 4. A repayment strategy"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"Pay the required minimum on every debt and direct all spare money to one. Close it, then add its payment to the next. Decide which comes first. Avalanche: sort by rate and repay the most expensive first, as emergency departments treat the most seriously ill rather than earliest arrivals. Mathematically, this minimises interest."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"Snowball: sort by balance and repay the smallest first. It costs a little more, but closes the first debt quickly, giving visible progress and energy to continue. Like the gym, early visible results sustain motivation better than promises."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"An example with 4 debts:"},{"id":"b019","markdown":"| Debt | Balance | Rate |\\n| Microfinance loan | 15 000 RUB | 0.8% daily |\\n| Credit card | 80 000 RUB | 48% annually |\\n| Consumer loan | 250 000 RUB | 22% annually |\\n| Appliance instalments | 30 000 RUB | 0% |\\nThe microloan goes first under either strategy: 0.8% daily is about 290% annually, and it is also smallest. Next comes the card at 48%. Snowball would put the 30 000 RUB instalments second, but repaying interest-free debt while the 48% card remains is too expensive."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"A practical approach is avalanche for everything above 30% annually. Among the rest, choose the method you can actually sustain."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"REPAYMENT ORDER. AVALANCHE BY RATE: MFO \xb7 0.8% DAILY, CARD \xb7 48%, LOAN \xb7 22%, INSTALMENTS \xb7 0%. SNOWBALL BY SIZE: 15 000 RUB MFO, 30 000 RUB INSTALMENTS, 80 000 RUB CARD, 250 000 RUB LOAN. USE AVALANCHE ABOVE 30% ANNUALLY. Four debts: which comes first?"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"Avalanche. Snowball. The lesson’s approach.\\n- 1 Microfinance loan: 15 000 RUB \xb7 0.8% daily \xb7 −3 650 RUB monthly.\\n- 2 Credit card: 80 000 RUB \xb7 48% annually \xb7 −3 200 RUB monthly.\\n- 3 Appliance instalments: 30 000 RUB \xb7 0% \xb7 0 RUB monthly.\\n- 4 Consumer loan: 250 000 RUB \xb7 22% annually \xb7 −4 583 RUB monthly. Use avalanche above 30% annually: repay the 0.8%-daily loan and 48% card first. For the rest, choose what you can sustain. The figures show monthly interest on current balances."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"### Step 5. Refinancing"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"Refinancing is a new, lower-rate loan used to repay old ones, sometimes consolidating several balances. Conditions may be favourable now. The key rate fell from a 21% peak in 2024–2025 to 14%, so a loan taken at the peak might already be refinanced more cheaply."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"It makes sense under 3 conditions: the new full cost is at least several percentage points lower; substantial time remains because early payments mostly consist of interest; and the term is not extended. The most common error is extending time: the rate falls, term lengthens, monthly payment drops, but total interest rises."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"Consumer loans may legally be repaid early without penalties, with advance notice to the bank, normally 30 days unless the contract specifies less. Many banks allow it in-app. When repaying early, choose a shorter term rather than a smaller payment to reduce interest more."},{"id":"b027","markdown":"Figures at the time of writing"},{"id":"b028","markdown":"Key rate on October 23: 14%. The text’s figures are dated to writing. Check current values on the official Bank of Russia website."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"### Step 6. If you cannot pay"},{"id":"b030","markdown":"The worst response is ignoring the bank and waiting for things to resolve. They will not: penalties, credit-history arrears, collectors and court proceedings follow."},{"id":"b031","markdown":"#### Call the bank before missing payment"},{"id":"b032","markdown":"Banks have restructuring programmes: longer terms, temporarily lower payments or deferrals. They prefer receiving money later to never receiving it, but usually offer help to people who approach them."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"#### Payment holidays"},{"id":"b034","markdown":"The law allows payment deferral or reduction for up to 6 months if income during the 2 months before application fell more than 30% against the previous year’s average. Consumer-loan limits apply: up to 450 000 RUB for cash loans and 150 000 RUB for cards. Each loan can receive a holiday once on the same grounds."},{"id":"b035","markdown":"Important: a holiday pauses payments; it does not forgive debt. Interest generally continues accruing and must later be paid."},{"id":"b036","markdown":"#### Your rights with collectors"},{"id":"b037","markdown":"The law limits collection. For one debt, calls may occur no more than 1 time daily, 2 weekly and 8 monthly, and never at night. Threats, pressure or revealing debt to colleagues and neighbours are also prohibited. Report violations to the Federal Bailiff Service, which supervises collectors."},{"id":"b038","markdown":"### Step 7. Bankruptcy as a last resort"},{"id":"b039","markdown":"If debt has become impossible to repay under any realistic circumstances, personal bankruptcy provides a legal discharge procedure."},{"id":"b040","markdown":"Out of court, through a multifunctional public-services centre, the process is free. It applies to total debt from 25 000 to 1 000 000 RUB when a statutory condition is met. Often bailiffs must have ended enforcement because there is nothing recoverable: no assets or income."},{"id":"b041","markdown":"Court bankruptcy is for larger amounts or people outside public-centre conditions. It costs money and takes months."},{"id":"b042","markdown":"Both have consequences. For several years you must disclose bankruptcy when seeking credit. Restrictions apply to management positions in companies, and you cannot immediately repeat the procedure. Bankruptcy is not a simple way to avoid paying; it is the final exit."},{"id":"b043","markdown":"Important: be cautious with companies advertising turnkey debt discharge. Some are honest lawyers; others collect advance fees and disappear. Before paying, check whether the free out-of-court procedure applies to you."},{"id":"b044","markdown":"### About shame"},{"id":"b045","markdown":"People often hide debt from partners, parents and friends. Shame makes them delay until options run out. Debt is a solvable financial situation and says nothing about your worth as a person. Acting earlier and telling people close to you preserves more options."},{"id":"b046","markdown":"### The essentials"},{"id":"b047","markdown":"- First get the full picture: credit history and a table of every debt."},{"id":"b048","markdown":"- Then turn off the tap: a voluntary restriction and no new debt."},{"id":"b049","markdown":"- Build a 1-month buffer, then direct all spare money towards one debt. Everything above 30% annually goes first."},{"id":"b050","markdown":"- Investigate refinancing, but do not extend the term."},{"id":"b051","markdown":"- If payment is impossible, approach the bank before arrears. Keep bankruptcy for the very last resort."},{"id":"b052","markdown":"Next lesson builds a financial cushion: how much, where to hold it and how to start from zero."}],"terms":[{"id":"dosrochnoe-pogashenie","term":"Early repayment","definition":"The best way to reduce loan overpayment. At a 21% rate, an early payment provides a guaranteed 21% “return” unavailable from investments."},{"id":"klyuchevaya-stavka","term":"Key interest rate","definition":"The rate at which the central bank lends to banks. The main monetary-policy tool and the economy’s “price of money”. A higher rate makes loans more expensive and slows inflation."},{"id":"kreditnaya-istoriya","term":"Credit history","definition":"A record of how you repaid loans. A poor or absent history means rejection or a rate above the market. An empty history is worse than a history of repaid loans."},{"id":"mfo","term":"Microfinance organisations (MFOs)","definition":"Companies lending to people who do not interest banks. After the banking-sector clean-up, their number in Russia exceeded 1000."},{"id":"psk","term":"Full cost of credit (PSK)","definition":"The equivalent of an effective interest rate: the real cost of a loan including every payment. Banks must disclose it, though in small print. The only honest comparison figure."},{"id":"refinansirovanie","term":"Refinancing","definition":"A new loan at a lower rate to repay an old one. It can help when the rate difference exceeds 1.5–2% and substantial time remains. It makes sense in the first half of the term."},{"id":"protsentnyy-punkt","term":"Percentage point","definition":"The difference between two percentages. A rate rising from 4% to 5% is up 1 percentage point, but up 25% relative to the rate itself. They are constantly confused, and dramatic headlines exploit that confusion."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"05-vyhod-iz-dolgov-q1","q":"You owe on a card at 48% and a consumer loan at 22%, with 10 000 RUB spare each month. Under avalanche, where does it go?","options":["Early repayment of the 48% card while paying the consumer-loan minimum","Equally to both debts","A deposit until debts disappear on their own"],"correct":0},{"id":"05-vyhod-iz-dolgov-q2","q":"You are offered a lower refinancing rate but a term 2 years longer. What matters?","options":["Nothing: a lower rate is always better","Only the new monthly payment","Total interest: the longer term can make it larger"],"correct":2},{"id":"05-vyhod-iz-dolgov-q3","q":"Income has fallen sharply and next month’s payment will be unaffordable. What is best?","options":["Stop answering bank calls","Approach the bank before arrears about restructuring or payment holidays","Take an MFO loan to make the payment"],"correct":1}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);